A||||2||Hartz||Calling all O2 sensor gurus||||5||
Z||000000||Hartz||04-25-2000||10:10 PM||||I have an '85 SE that into which I have placed a 3.1L turbo V6.  I have always been a bit suspicious of the programming that came with the PROM in the turbo kit, so tonight I installed an Air/Fuel meter.  <P>Now I am familiar with the general concepts of oxygen sensors, but the meter is telling me things that I didn't necessarily anticipate.  This thing is jumping all over the board!  I can verify all 15 LEDs on this thing work!  The guage will show lean(stoichiometric value of around 17) to rich (around 12)and anywhere in between depending on throttle position.  <P>I would have thought it would have been a little more stable (say maybe a range of 13-15 or so).  <P>Am I wrong or is my ECM really whacked?<P>Any thoughts appreciated.<P>Thanks,<BR>Hartz||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cooter||04-25-2000||10:48 PM||||Your ecm and other stuff is ok. In order to maintain a decent mixture, the ecm swings the mixture from lean to rich. Be very worried if the meter shows a lean condition under full throttle.||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||04-25-2000||10:59 PM||||1. The O2 is not linear. The system is called a bang-bang controller. The O2 will read .456V at 14.7:1, but when lean, it will read somewhere below .456V, but where is unknown. Same for rich. It will read above .456V, but where is unknown. Given this, the ECM swings the fuel rich and lean in an attempt to evenly cross this .456V. The result is that the average AFR is 14.7:1.<BR>That's why the Fuel Status swings between RICH and LEAN on a scanner.<P>2. The jumping all over is correct. The O2 is reading the changes in the injector pulse width. <P>3. Slight changes in the throttle position will change the injector pulse width, so the O2 meter will move around. At WOT, the injectors increase to a fixed pulse width so the gauge will tend to stablize. The second you let off, it will go full lean (deceleration enleanment) and then pop back into closed loop (cycling).<P>4. There is no way to know the true AFR at WOT, so there is a target of 12:1 in the code. GM calc's this based on the injector size and pulse width. Yep, it's an educated guess.<P>5. The O2 gauge is good for an overview of the fuel mixture, but useless for absolute values (except at .456V where it will be 14.7:1). <P>6. For the turbo engine, you should depend more on a scanner and watch for WOT O2 reading that drop below .8V. On my SC engine, I checked three O2's and used the one that read the lowest (safety margin). Short of having a EGT, this is best safety margin.<P>Everything sounds normal. I would be curious to see what your BLM and INT are at cruise and idle. That will tell if the fueling is right. At WOT, the O2 voltage is the one to watch. If you start getting into the .7V or lower range, it's likely getting too lean. But again, what the actual AFR is requires an expensive linear O2 and they won't work with the GM ECM.<P>Is your ECM a 6869 or 7170?<BR>TK<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 04-25-2000).]||||reg||1
